  "selected_policy": {
    "trace_cap": 16,
    "basis": "Cap 16 is the measured knee. It is 1.0455x faster in whole-wave wall throughput than cap 64, reduces retained trace candidates from 97 to 24, and lowers summed concurrent curve time by 14.45%. Cap 1 is only 0.04 seconds faster over the fixed ten-curve wave, a noise-scale 0.015%, while requiring 11 more SEA levels and 1.08% more summed curve time than cap 16.",
    "production_interpretation": "A ten-curve range with curve_threads=10 is one fixed initial wave and never exercises rolling refill. Long production continuously refills freed slots, so the cap-16 advantage in summed task work is operationally relevant even though cap 1 tied it in this single-wave wall measurement."
  },
  "time_wrapper_note": {
    "wrapper": "/usr/bin/time -l",
    "diagnostic_in_all_four_logs": "time: sysctl kern.clockrate: Operation not permitted",
    "interpretation": "The blocked post-run macOS sysctl does not indicate an incomplete search: all logs contain complete summaries, all checkpoints end at next_index 22, and real/user/sys times were printed. Peak RSS comes from search progress."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "Each run is a single ten-curve wave with exactly ten curve threads. Its wall time is dominated by the slowest overlapping work and does not directly measure steady-state rolling throughput.",
    "Summed per-curve total_us is concurrent task time, not invocation wall time. It is useful for comparing work and rolling-pool pressure but still includes contention and host-load effects.",
    "Ten curves are too few for a confidence interval. The 0.04-second cap-1 wall advantage over cap 16 is treated as noise, not evidence of superior throughput.",
    "All forty observations ended in sound smoothness rejection and produced zero certificates, so this ablation measures search work and stopping behavior rather than empirical certificate yield.",
    "These bounded checkpoints are not production continuation authority."
  ]
